    summary: 'Speculative decoding attacks the memory-bound nature of decode. Instead of one target forward pass per token, a small drafter guesses several tokens ahead and the target model scores all of them in a single batched forward pass. A rejection-sampling rule accepts a prefix of the guesses so that the final tokens are distributed exactly as if the target had sampled them itself.',
    purpose: 'Reduces the number of sequential target forward passes per generated token, which lowers inter-token latency when decode is bandwidth-bound and the accelerator has spare compute.',
    mechanics: 'Each round: the drafter produces `k` candidate tokens `x_1..x_k` with draft probabilities `q(x_i)`. The target runs one forward pass over the prompt plus all `k` candidates and yields target distributions `p` at all `k + 1` positions. Candidates are checked left to right: accept `x_i` with probability `min(1, p(x_i) / q(x_i))`. On the first rejection at position `i`, sample a replacement from the residual `norm(max(0, p - q))` and stop. If all `k` are accepted, sample one bonus token from the target distribution at position `k + 1`. Each round therefore emits between 1 and `k + 1` tokens for one target pass; the formal guarantee is that the output matches the target sampling distribution. The expected tokens per round is roughly `(1 - a^(k+1)) / (1 - a)` for a per-token acceptance rate `a` when acceptances are independent. Wall-clock speedup then depends on draft cost per token, `k`, `a`, target pass cost and whether the runtime can run verification without breaking continuous batching. Greedy decoding uses the simpler exact-match rule. The KV cache for rejected positions must be rolled back, which is why paged KV engines track speculative slots explicitly.',
