    mechanics: 'Weight memory is approximately `params × bits_per_weight / 8` plus scale overhead: a 70B model is about 140 GB at 16 bits, about 70 GB at 8 bits and about 35 GB at 4 bits. Per-tensor, per-channel or per-group scales (group size 64 to 128 is common for INT4) map integers back to real values: `w ≈ s × (q − z)`. Post-training methods choose those integers to minimize output error on a small calibration set: GPTQ solves it layer by layer with second-order information, AWQ scales salient channels identified by activation magnitude before rounding, SmoothQuant migrates activation outliers into the weights to make W8A8 tractable. FP8 (E4M3 for weights and activations, often E5M2 for gradients) is a floating format with per-tensor or per-block scaling and is supported natively by recent tensor cores. Quantization-aware training fine-tunes with simulated quantization to recover accuracy when PTQ is insufficient. At runtime, weight-only kernels dequantize inside the matmul; their speedup comes from reading fewer bytes and appears at small batch sizes, while at large batch sizes the matmul is compute-bound and the dequant work can make it slower than BF16. Smaller weight files therefore do not guarantee proportional latency gains; the result depends on the kernel, the hardware, the batch size and the model shape. Distinct from KV-cache quantization, which addresses a different memory pool.',
