    mechanics: 'For conventional attention `KV bytes ≈ 2 × layers × tokens × KV_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element × sequences`; moving from BF16 (2 bytes) to FP8 or INT8 (1 byte) halves that. Each stored element is scaled: `k_q = round(k / s_k)` with `s_k` held per tensor, per layer and head, or per token and group. FP8 E4M3 keeps more mantissa and is the usual choice for K and V; E5M2 trades mantissa for range. INT8 needs calibrated scales, and INT4 KV schemes usually apply per-channel quantization to keys and per-token quantization to values because key channels carry outliers. Attention kernels must read the quantized blocks, dequantize into registers and compute scores in higher precision; FlashAttention and FlashInfer variants support FP8 KV on recent hardware. Precision risk accumulates with context length because every score over a long history sees quantization error, so the accuracy cost is workload dependent and should be measured on long-context evaluations. MLA architectures cache a compressed latent instead of full K and V, which changes the memory model and interacts differently with quantization.',
