    summary: 'KV offloading extends the KV pool with slower tiers. Prefix-cache blocks that were evicted from HBM, and sequences that were preempted mid-generation, can be parked in host memory or on NVMe and brought back instead of being recomputed. Whether that pays off depends on transfer bandwidth versus recompute cost.',
    purpose: 'Raises prefix-cache hit rate and reduces recompute on preemption when HBM is the scarce resource and the interconnect has bandwidth to spare.',
    mechanics: 'The engine keeps its paged KV blocks in HBM and, on eviction or preemption, copies whole blocks to a host-pinned buffer (tier 1) and optionally to local NVMe or a remote KV store (tier 2). A lookup key derived from the block hash locates blocks on any tier. On a prefix hit in a lower tier, blocks are copied back over PCIe (in the tens of GB/s per GPU, hardware dependent) before prefill resumes from the first missing block. Break-even is a bandwidth comparison: restoring a block is worth it when `block_bytes / transfer_bandwidth` is below the prefill time to recompute it, which favors offload for long prefixes on large models and disfavors it for short prompts or small models. Transfers are overlapped with compute using copy streams and layer-wise pipelining so the GPU is not idle while blocks arrive. Preempted sequences can be swapped out entirely and swapped back in (vLLM-style swap) or simply recomputed; recompute is often cheaper for short sequences. Remote or shared stores add network latency and require consistent block hashing across engines; this is one strategy among several and its benefit is deployment specific.',
