    summary: 'A decode step for a large model launches hundreds of small kernels, and at small batch sizes the CPU time to enqueue them can exceed the GPU time to run them. CUDA Graphs record that launch sequence once per shape bucket and replay it as a single submission, so the GPU stays fed.',
    purpose: 'Removes host-side launch overhead from the decode loop, which is the difference between being GPU-bound and being CPU-bound at low batch sizes.',
    mechanics: 'During warmup the engine runs a decode step for each supported batch size (for example 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, up to a cap) under stream capture; every kernel launch, memory copy and NCCL collective on that stream is recorded into a graph with fixed pointers and shapes. At runtime a step with batch size `b` is padded up to the nearest captured bucket and the graph is replayed with one launch. Because pointers are baked in, all inputs must live in preallocated static buffers that the runtime fills before replay, and paged KV addressing must be expressed through block tables in those buffers rather than through pointer changes. Dynamic control flow, host synchronizations and shape-dependent kernels break capture, which is why prefill (variable sequence lengths, large shapes, already GPU-bound) commonly runs eagerly while decode replays graphs. Piecewise capture is one strategy that graphs everything except the attention kernel, which then handles dynamic lengths itself. Capture has a warmup cost in time and memory per bucket, and every distinct configuration (TP degree, LoRA count, speculation length) multiplies the number of graphs.',
