    summary: 'Kernel compilation turns the model graph into fused GPU kernels specialized for the target hardware, dtypes and shapes. It removes intermediate memory round trips (for example norm plus residual add plus quantize in one kernel) and picks tile sizes by autotuning, at the cost of build time and shape brittleness.',
    purpose: 'Lowers per-step time and memory traffic beyond what generic library calls deliver, particularly for elementwise chains and custom attention or MoE paths.',
    mechanics: 'Three approaches are common. torch.compile traces the PyTorch graph (Dynamo), lowers it to TorchInductor and emits Triton kernels for fused elementwise and reduction chains while dispatching matmuls to cuBLAS or generated GEMMs; it specializes on shapes and recompiles on new dynamic dimensions unless they are marked dynamic. Hand-written Triton or CUDA kernels (paged attention, fused MoE dispatch and GEMM, RMSNorm plus quantize, RoPE plus KV write) are shipped by engines and selected by hardware and dtype. TensorRT-LLM builds an engine ahead of time: a fixed plan of kernels for a maximum batch, sequence length and precision, with autotuned tactics per GEMM shape, which yields fast steady-state execution but a long build and no flexibility outside the compiled ranges. All approaches add a warmup phase at startup and benefit from a persistent compile cache so replicas do not rebuild. Fusion matters most where decode is bandwidth-bound: a fused kernel reads and writes activations once instead of several times. Numerical results can differ slightly from eager execution because of reordered reductions and different accumulation precision.',
