    keywords: ['KV transfer', 'NVLink', 'RDMA', 'NIXL', 'Mooncake', 'llm-d', 'Dynamo', 'TTFT vs ITL', 'goodput', 'vLLM disaggregated'],
    summary: 'Prefill is compute-bound and batches well over long prompts; decode is bandwidth-bound and wants many concurrent sequences with small per-step work. Disaggregated serving assigns each phase to its own pool of engines, transfers the prompt KV cache from the prefill engine to a decode engine, and lets each pool be sized, parallelized and scheduled for its own regime.',
    purpose: 'Removes the interference where a long prefill stalls the decode steps of every other sequence on the same GPU, so TTFT and inter-token latency can be met with separate SLOs and separate hardware.',
    mechanics: 'A request enters a prefill engine, which computes the prompt KV (respecting prefix-cache hits) and emits the first token. The KV blocks are then transferred to a decode engine over NVLink within a node, RDMA or InfiniBand across nodes, or plain network in the worst case; the transfer size is `KV bytes` for the prompt, on the order of tens of MB to a few GB per request depending on model and length. Transfer can be layer-wise, overlapped with prefill, so the decode engine begins as soon as the last layer arrives. The decode pool then runs continuous batching over many sequences at high concurrency. Because pools are separate, prefill can use a higher TP degree or larger chunk sizes, and decode can use a different TP or EP layout and more KV memory. Orchestration must pair engines, track KV location, handle transfer failures, and rebalance when the phase mix shifts. This is not automatically superior: chunked prefill on a colocated engine addresses much of the same interference at zero transfer cost, and disaggregation costs transfer bandwidth, added TTFT for the hop, queueing between pools, reduced prefix-cache locality and more moving parts. It tends to win for long prompts, strict ITL SLOs and large fleets.',
    input: 'Prompt tokens and any cached prefix on the prefill side; KV blocks plus request state on the decode side.',
    output: 'First token from prefill; the remaining stream from decode.',
    tensorShape: 'Transferred KV per request: [layers, prompt_blocks, block_size, H_kv, d_head] × 2 in the KV dtype.',
    memory: 'Prefill pool holds few long sequences briefly; decode pool holds many sequences for their full lifetime, so its KV pool is the larger one.',
    compute: 'Prefill GPUs run at high utilization on large matmuls; decode GPUs run bandwidth-bound steps and can use cheaper or different accelerators in some designs.',
    communication: 'One bulk KV transfer per request between pools; bandwidth and latency of that link bound the added TTFT and the request rate the design can sustain.',
    knobs: ['Prefill to decode GPU ratio', 'Transfer transport (NVLink, RDMA, TCP) and chunking / layer-wise overlap', 'Per-pool parallelism (TP / PP / EP) and KV dtype', 'Routing policy for which decode engine receives the KV', 'Fallback to colocated serving when transfer capacity is exhausted', 'Prefix-cache placement across pools'],
    metrics: ['TTFT including transfer hop', 'ITL / TPOT on the decode pool', 'KV transfer bandwidth and per-request transfer time', 'Goodput: requests meeting both TTFT and ITL SLOs per GPU', 'Per-pool utilization and queue depth'],
