    name: 'Multi-LoRA serving',
    subtitle: 'One resident base model plus many selectable low-rank adapters, batched together with adapter-aware kernels.',
    subsystem: 'serving',
    modes: ['system', 'inference'],
    aliases: ['LoRA serving', 'adapter serving', 'S-LoRA', 'Punica', 'multi-tenant fine-tunes'],
    keywords: ['S-LoRA', 'Punica', 'SGMV', 'BGMV', 'adapter cache', 'unmerged LoRA', 'vLLM LoRA', 'LoRAX', 'PEFT', 'per-request adapter'],
    summary: 'Multi-LoRA serving keeps the base weights resident once and applies a per-request LoRA delta on top, so thousands of fine-tuned variants can share one GPU fleet. Requests with different adapters run in the same batch; custom kernels gather each row’s adapter so the batch does not have to be split by adapter.',
    purpose: 'Makes per-customer or per-task fine-tunes economical by amortizing the base model across all of them instead of deploying one full replica per variant.',
    mechanics: 'A LoRA adapter contributes `ΔW = B A` with rank `r`, so for input `x` the layer computes `W x + B (A x)` without merging. In a heterogeneous batch, each request carries an adapter id; a segmented or batched gather-matmul kernel (S-LoRA and Punica style SGMV / BGMV) applies the correct `A` and `B` slices per row in one launch, with cost roughly `2 × r × (d_in + d_out)` extra FLOPs per token per adapted matrix. Adapter weights live in a GPU adapter cache with paged storage of `A` and `B` (rank varies by adapter), backed by host memory and disk; a request whose adapter is not resident pays a load before it can be scheduled. The base model is unchanged, so prefix-cache blocks must include the adapter identity in their key, since the same prompt yields different KV under different adapters. Merged adapters are only viable for a single variant; unmerged application costs the extra low-rank matmul but keeps one base. LoRA does not make inference faster; it makes many variants cheap to host.',
    input: 'Batch of requests, each with an adapter id (or none); base weights; resident adapter tensors.',
    output: 'Base plus adapter output for every row, produced in one batched step.',
