    summary: 'Autoscaling for LLM serving decides how many replicas of each model to run. Request count is a poor signal because one request may cost 100 tokens or 100,000; useful signals are queue depth, running batch size, KV utilization and observed TTFT against the SLO. The dominant constraint is cold-start time: a new replica must fetch and load tens to hundreds of GB of weights, warm up kernels and graphs, and only then admit traffic.',
    purpose: 'Keeps latency SLOs met under changing load while not paying for idle accelerators, given that capacity cannot be added in seconds.',
    mechanics: 'A controller reads per-replica metrics (waiting requests, running sequences, KV pool utilization, TTFT and ITL percentiles) on a short interval and computes desired replicas from a target such as `queue_time_p95 < budget` or `kv_util < 0.85`. Scale-up lead time is the sum of scheduling a GPU node, pulling the image, loading weights (bounded by storage or network bandwidth; a 70B BF16 model is about 140 GB), and warmup (CUDA graph capture, compile cache, prefix priming), which is commonly minutes; therefore the controller must scale on a leading indicator and hold headroom rather than react to saturation. Scale-down uses connection draining so in-flight generations complete, and a cooldown to avoid flapping. Warm pools, pre-pulled weights on local NVMe, and fast weight loaders (safetensors with parallel reads, peer-to-peer weight sharing between replicas) shorten the lead time. Multi-model fleets also need model-level decisions: which model to evict from a GPU to make room for another. Backpressure covers the gap during scale-up.',
