    summary: 'Every serving decision is a trade between a few well-defined quantities. Latency splits into queue time, time to first token and per-token time; throughput is tokens per second per GPU; efficiency signals include KV utilization, batch occupancy and prefix-cache hit rate; cost closes the loop. Precise definitions matter because the same words are often used for different measurements.',
    purpose: 'Gives the fleet one vocabulary so that scheduler, quantization, speculation and routing changes can be compared and SLOs can be stated unambiguously.',
    mechanics: 'Queue time: from admission to the first scheduler iteration that includes the request. TTFT: from request receipt to the first output token leaving the engine (includes queue time and prefill; specify whether gateway hops are included). TPOT (time per output token) and ITL (inter-token latency): per-token time after the first; TPOT is usually reported as `(E2E − TTFT) / (output_tokens − 1)` and ITL as the distribution of gaps between consecutive tokens, which exposes stalls that an average hides. E2E latency: receipt to final token. Throughput: output tokens per second per GPU, or total tokens per second including prompt tokens when prefill dominates; always state batch size and prompt / output length distribution. Goodput: requests per second that meet both TTFT and ITL SLOs. KV utilization: used KV blocks over total; batch occupancy: running sequences over the configured maximum; prefix-cache hit rate: cached prompt tokens over total prompt tokens. GPU utilization from NVML only says a kernel was resident and must be read alongside HBM bandwidth utilization, SM or tensor-core occupancy and communication time, because a bandwidth-bound decode step shows 100% utilization while using a fraction of the FLOPs. Cost per request is GPU-seconds times price divided by requests; cost per successful task additionally divides by the task success rate, so a slower but more accurate model can be cheaper per task. Report percentiles (p50, p95, p99), not means.',
